-- §7  Which buffers the corpus actually sizes, and the one that reads zero.
#
# ONE ROW PER (CORPUS LAYER, BUFFER): whether that buffer was SIZED, and the typed absence if
# not. A bound is worth exactly as much as the rows it has to bound, so this IS the coverage
# for the two inequalities built on `S`.
#
# ⭐ `Σ_{j ≠ unrealised} H[l,j] ≤ S[l, A(l)]` bounds the filed shares against the slack of the
# buffer the remainder names as its absorber, and the `capacity` rows close a second inequality
# on top of that, the one bounding what a filing's own demand and nameplate say could have
# gone unserved.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE INTERESTING ANSWER IS THE ONE THAT READS ZERO, AND IT NOW READS ZERO A DIFFERENT WAY.
# Eleven corpus layers size their capacity slack and every one sizes it at [0,0,0], a filer
# saying the supply cannot be run above its rating at any price. That is the TIGHTEST possible
# bound rather than a missing one, so the shortfall inequality is exercised, not silent.
#
# ⚠️ THIS PARAGRAPH SAID THE OPPOSITE UNTIL 2026-09-05, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF SAYING SO. It
# read "no layer in this corpus sizes its capacity slack" and was true when written; then the
# `absent reason="none"` slacks became claimed zeros and the sentence did not move, because
# nothing printed the number beside it. examples/observations.rs prints it now. A bound with
# nothing to bound still passes loudest, the trap is real, it is just not this column today.
#
# ⚠️ A NULL `absent` BESIDE AN UNSIZED ROW IS NOT THE SAME AS `unmeasured`. The schema's CHECK
# constraints decide which combinations are legal; this query only reports what is there.
-- entries/slacks.sqlc at corpus scope.
SELECT s.filing            AS "filing!",
       s.layer             AS "layer!",
       s.buffer::text      AS "buffer!",
       s.sized             AS "sized!",
       s.absent::text      AS "absent"
FROM (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s
JOIN (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) f USING (filing)
ORDER BY s.filing, s.layer, s.buffer
